AU  - Dong, ZY
AU  - Wei, Q
AU  - Chen, XH
AU  - Luo, YM
TI  - Are gamers happier? Multidimensional well-being differences in risk groups for problematic internet use and internet gaming disorder
T2  - PERSONALITY AND INDIVIDUAL DIFFERENCES
AB  - Overuse of the internet has become a global problem that lowers well-being. However, differences in well-being and their interrelationships between risk groups of Internet Gaming Disorder (IGD) and Problematic Internet Use (PIU) remain unclear, and interventions for these groups' well-being are still lacking. This study recruited 6765 college students (Mage = 18.37 years) who completed questionnaires on IGD, PIU, and well-being (life satisfaction, flourishing, psychological richness). Data analysis was conducted using latent profile analysis, ANOVA, and network analysis. The results showed that participants were divided into three groups: moderate PIU and IGD risk, high PIU risk, and high IGD risk. The IGD risk group exhibited higher overall well-being and stronger associations in the well-being network compared to the PIU risk group. This suggests that online gaming is more effective than other forms of internet use in maintaining overall well-being. However, the IGD group may have heightened emotional sensitivity and more difficulty in emotional regulation. Additionally, life satisfaction and novel experiences were identified as the most effective intervention targets for enhancing overall well-being in the IGD and PIU risk groups, respectively. This study provides new insights for developing well-being enhancement strategies tailored to different patterns of internet use.
