AU  - Duc, TM
TI  - Excessive social media use and its impact on psychological well-being and academic performance of Vietnamese university students
T2  - INTERNATIONAL JOURNAL OF ADOLESCENCE AND YOUTH
AB  - This research focuses on social media overuse consequences on psychological well-being and academic performance of university students in Vietnam through the Stressor-Strain-Outcome (SSO) model. The author applies the Facebook Addiction Scale, DASS-21 and self-reported GPAs for data collection comprising 300 respondents from Hanoi and Ho Chi Minh City. Measurement and structural model validation were performed using PLS-SEM. The findings show that higher social media overuse contributes to increased levels of psychological distress/anxiety (beta = 0.52, p < .001) and a deterioration in academic performance (beta = -0.31, p < .001). There is a partial mediating effect of stress and anxiety on the performance outcomes which indicates a psychological strain in the social media overuse and academic performance relationship (indirect beta = -0.27, p < .001). Self-regulation also significantly influenced the stress-academic performance relationship (beta = 0.18, p = .002) as a moderator. This research contributes to the application of the SSO model and calls for the need for more digital literacy, self-regulated learning, effective time management, and mental health support in the Vietnamese higher education system.
