TI  - Evaluation of the Gender Minority Stress Model in Transgender and Gender Diverse College Students
T2  - PSYCHOLOGY OF SEXUAL ORIENTATION AND GENDER DIVERSITY
AB  - Transgender and gender diverse (TGD) college students report experiencing significantly more psychological distress compared to their cisgender peers. The gender minority stress resilience (GMSR) model offers a theoretical rationale for why, suggesting that experiencing more distal (external) stress indirectly contributes to worse health outcomes by exacerbating proximal (internal) stress. Using latent variable structural equation modeling, the present study investigated the indirect effect of distal stress on depression, anxiety, and posttraumatic stress disorder (PTSD) symptoms via proximal stress among a sample of n = 234 TGD college students recruited via social media. Participants were highly distressed, as 50.8% exceeded the cutoff for severe depression symptoms and 53.4% exceeded the cutoff for severe anxiety symptoms. Of the 59.8% of the sample who reported experiencing at least one Criterion A trauma, 82.3% exceeded the cutoff for probable PTSD (49.1% of the entire sample). Experiencing more distal stress was associated with more PTSD and anxiety symptoms after accounting for proximal stress, and distal stress predicted proximal stress. There was a significant indirect path from distal stress to depression symptoms via proximal stress. Results suggest that interventions targeting proximal stress may help reduce depression symptoms among TGD college students, while systemic efforts to minimize distal stress may have the strongest benefit for PTSD and anxiety symptoms. Future research advancing the GMSR model among college students should utilize longitudinal designs as well as assess additional proximal stressors and other forms of trauma faced by TGD college students. Public Significance Statement Transgender and gender diverse (TGD) college students may experience numerous stressors and traumas specifically related to their gender. As these experiences accumulate, they may have harmful impacts on the mental health of TGD students.
