TI  - Active and passive social media use associated with different daily affects: A daily diary study
T2  - PERSONALITY AND INDIVIDUAL DIFFERENCES
AB  - Social media use (SMU) is increasingly prevalent among university students, but the dynamic relationship between different forms of SMU and daily affects remain unclear. This daily diary study examined the associations between active and passive SMU with daily positive affect, boredom, depression-related, and anxiety-related affects. 394 undergraduate students (18.27% male, Mage = 19.88, SD = 1.06) ultimately completed a 21-day daily survey. The results revealed distinct associations between active and passive SMU with daily effects. Specifically, active SMU was positively associated with daily positive affect and negatively associated with depression-related affect. In contrast, passive SMU was positively associated with depression-related and anxietyrelated affects. Moreover, active SMU was also negatively correlated with boredom, whereas passive SMU showed the opposite effect. These findings suggest that how students engage with social media may be more informative than overall use in understanding daily affects. These findings offer valuable implications for understanding the relationship between SMU and mental health among university students.
