AU  - Barth, GM
AU  - Renner, T
AU  - Batra, A
AU  - Brandhorst, I
TI  - Preparation of an Online Training Programme for the Parents of Adolescents and Young Adults with Internet Use Disorders
T2  - KINDHEIT UND ENTWICKLUNG
AB  - Theoretical background: According to current research, parents can influence both the development and the course of internet use disorders (IUD) like gaming disorders (GD) or social network use disorders (SUD) in their children. Nevertheless, there are few evidence-based treatment options for the target group, especially no e-mental health options. Objective: What are the needs of parents who consider their child's internet use problematic have for an online training program? Method: 217 parents (79 % mothers) of adolescents and young adults were recruited for an anonymous online survey through social networks, a project website, and a flyer between 10/2020 and 7/2021. A total of 165 datasets of parents were analyzed regarding the IUD symptoms of their children, using an adapted parental version of the Compulsive Internet Use Scale, as well as the parents' problems, demands, and wishes for an online training program (e. g., the extent, duration, or level of personal contact). Results: 57 % of parents indicated an interest in an online training program. The degree of perceived problems was the strongest predictor for parental willingness to participate. 82 % of the parents considered personal contact during online training program to be important or very important for them. Discussion and conclusion: The online survey results showed relevant parental interest in an online training program. Consequently, a corresponding program was developed for parents of young persons with GD or SUD in accordance with the results of the online survey, the current research, and clinical experience. The training outlines are being introduced.
