TY  - JOUR
AU  - Zhang, JJ
AU  - Wang, EN
TI  - Heterogeneous patterns of problematic smartphone use and depressive symptoms among college students: understanding the role of self-compassion
T2  - CURRENT PSYCHOLOGY
AB  - The co-occurrence of problematic smartphone use and depressive symptoms in college students has drawn considerable attention. However, prior research has primarily adopted variable-centered approaches and overlooked the heterogeneous patterns among groups concerning these two issues. In our study with 763 Chinese undergraduates, we adopted a person-centered approach to explore this issue, using problematic smartphone use and depressive symptoms as indicators to identify distinct latent groups and examine their relation to individual self-compassion. Latent profile analysis identified three latent classes: low-symptom group (21.76%), medium PSU-risk group (58.45%), and severe co-occurrence group (19.79%), confirming the heterogeneous co-occurrence patterns of problematic smartphone use and depressive symptoms. Furthermore, multinomial logistic regression revealed that these distinct latent subgroups can be differentiated by the three components of self-compassion. Mindfulness acted as a protective factor for both the medium PSU-risk group and the severe co-occurrence group compared to the low-symptom group, while the other two components of self-compassion-self-kindness and common humanity-were particularly effective in differentiating the severe co-occurrence group from the low-symptom group. Our findings contribute to the understanding of the complex relationship between problematic smartphone use and depressive symptoms and deepen our understanding of how the three elements of self-compassion function. They provide insights into developing tailored prevention and intervention strategies to reduce problematic smartphone use and depressive symptoms, especially in college students. Future research should explore the longitudinal effects of three components of self-compassion on these two mental health issues and consider the impact of cultural differences.
