AU  - Suh, KH
TI  - The mediating effects of self-presentational motives and body dissatisfaction between histrionic personality and selfie addiction in female college students
T2  - CURRENT PSYCHOLOGY
AB  - This study explores the relationship between histrionic personality traits and selfie addiction by focusing on the mediating effects of self-presentational motives and body dissatisfaction. The sample consisted of 249 female Korean college students aged 18-31 years (M = 22.76, SD= 2.53), all of whom were active smartphone users. Data were collected using the Korean Personality Disorder Test, Self-presentational Motivation Scale, Body Esteem Scale, and Selfie Addiction Tendency Scale. The parallel mediating effect was analyzed using PROCESS Macro 4.2 Model 4. The results revealed that histrionic personality traits were positively correlated with self-presentational motives and body dissatisfaction but negatively correlated with selfie addiction. Self-presentational motives were positively correlated with selfie addiction, whereas body dissatisfaction was negatively correlated with selfie addiction. No significant correlation was found between self-presentational motives and body dissatisfaction. Although both self-presentational motives and body dissatisfaction were statistically significant mediators, their indirect effects operated in opposite directions. Although both self-presentational motives and body dissatisfaction were statistically significant mediators, they operated in opposite directions, resulting in a negligible total indirect effect. Notably, in the parallel mediation model, histrionic personality positively associated with selfie addiction through self-presentational motives and negatively associated with selfie addiction through body dis-satisfaction among female college students. These results suggest that self-presentation motives and body dissatisfaction are key considerations for mental health professionals when addressing selfie addiction among individuals with strong histrionic personality traits.
